const body = `<h1>4.5c &#8212; String Analysis with Loops</h1>

<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Expand Application. You've printed shapes with nested
    loops &#8212; now apply loops to something practical: tearing apart
    Strings character by character.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>Build a String Analyzer that takes a word or sentence and counts
    vowels, reverses the text, and checks for palindromes &#8212; all by
    looping over individual characters.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">Enter a word: racecar
Length: 7
Vowels: 3
Reversed: racecar
Palindrome? YES!</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Build It: String Analyzer</h3>

    <p>Create <code>StringAnalyzer.java</code>. Start with this skeleton:</p>

    <pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;

public class StringAnalyzer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter a word: ");
        String word = input.nextLine();

        System.out.println("Length: " + word.length());

        // TASK 1: Count vowels
        int vowels = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            char c = word.charAt(i);
            // YOUR CODE: check if c is a vowel
        }
        System.out.println("Vowels: " + vowels);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker Challenge &#8212; Reverse It</h3>
        <p>Add a loop that builds the reversed version of the word.
        Start with an empty String: <code>String reversed = "";</code>
        Then loop from the END of the word to the beginning, adding
        each character. Don't look up the formula &#8212; think about what
        index the last character is at.</p>
        <p><strong>Do NOT use StringBuilder.reverse().</strong> Build it
        character by character. You'll understand loops better.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <p>When you loop from the end to the beginning, what index do
        you start at? Hint: <code>word.length()</code> is 7 for "racecar",
        but the last character is at index 6. If your loop starts at
        <code>word.length()</code>, you get a StringIndexOutOfBoundsException.
        Why?</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Palindrome Check</h3>

    <p>Now that you have the reversed string, checking for a palindrome
    is trivial:</p>

    <pre><code>if (word.equals(reversed)) {
    System.out.println("Palindrome? YES!");
} else {
    System.out.println("Palindrome? No.");
}</code></pre>

    <p>But wait &#8212; try "Racecar" with a capital R. Does it still work?
    Fix it so case doesn't matter.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does <code>word.charAt(word.length())</code> crash
                but <code>word.charAt(word.length() - 1)</code> doesn't?</li>
            <li>Compare your two loops: the forward loop (counting vowels)
                and the backward loop (reversing). What's the same? What
                single thing changes between them?</li>
            <li>How would you count only LOWERCASE vowels? What changes?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Extend It (pick one)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Count consonants too. Print a vowel-to-consonant ratio.</li>
            <li>Handle full sentences with spaces: ignore spaces when
                checking for palindromes ("a man a plan a canal panama").</li>
            <li>Count how many times each letter appears (frequency
                analysis). You'll need a way to track 26 counts.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

